{"word": "glory", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "glory", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡlɔː.ɹi/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡloɹ.i/", "tags": ["Canada", "General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈɡlɔɹ.i]", "tags": ["Canada", "General American", "dialectal"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡloː.ɹi/", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈɡlo(ː)ɹi/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "glories", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "Great beauty and splendor.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "He was thinking; but the glory of the song, the swell from the great organ, the clustered lights, […], the height and vastness of this noble fane, its antiquity and its strength—all these things seemed to have their part as causes of the thrilling emotion that accompanied his thoughts.", "reference": "1918, W[illiam] B[abington] Maxwell, chapter V, in The Mirror and the Lamp, Indianapolis, Ind.: The Bobbs-Merrill Company, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "One of the hidden glories of Victorian engineering is proper drains. Isolating a city’s effluent and shipping it away in underground sewers has probably saved more lives than any medical procedure except vaccination.", "reference": "2014 June 14, “It's a gas”, in The Economist, volume 411, number 8891:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "Honour, admiration, or distinction, accorded by common consent to a person or thing; high reputation; renown.", "tags": ["countable", "uncountable"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "In this faire wize they traueild long yfere,\nThrough many hard assayes, which did betide;\nOf which he honour still away did beare,\nAnd spred his glorie through all countries wide.", "reference": "1590, Edmund Spenser, “Book II